    <h1>What Is the Norse Word for Raven?</h1>
  </header>
  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Old Norse word for raven is <strong><em>hrafn</em></strong> (plural <em>hrafnar</em>). It is a masculine noun of the first declension, cognate with Old English <em>hræfn</em>, Old High German <em>hraban</em>, and modern German <em>Rabe</em>. The word is the source of the name Odin's ravens (<em>Huginn</em> and <em>Muninn</em>), personal names like Hrafnkel, and many Scandinavian and Icelandic place names.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Word: <em>hrafn</em></h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Old Norse <em>hrafn</em> is recorded in the major reference works for the language. Geir Zoëga's <em>Concise Dictionary of Old Icelandic</em> (1910) gives: <em>hrafn</em>, m. — raven. The plural is <em>hrafnar</em> (nominative), <em>hrafna</em> (genitive plural, as in <em>hrafna-guð</em>, "raven-god" — an Odin byname).<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>

    <table class="qa-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Old Norse</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Singular nominative</td><td><em>hrafn</em></td><td>Standard form</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plural nominative</td><td><em>hrafnar</em></td><td>"the ravens"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Genitive plural</td><td><em>hrafna</em></td><td>Used in compounds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Genitive singular</td><td><em>hrafns</em></td><td>e.g. in personal names</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Pronunciation</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Old Norse orthography uses <em>hr-</em> to indicate a voiceless (aspirated) r-sound — a sound that no longer exists in modern English. The initial sequence is a strongly breathed H followed immediately by R, then the vowel <em>a</em> (short, as in "father"), then <em>vn</em> (the <em>f</em> in Old Norse was pronounced as a <em>v</em> between vowels and before voiced consonants).</p>
    <p>A reasonable modern approximation: <strong>HRAVN</strong> (rhymes approximately with "drawn" but with a breathy H-R onset). Modern Icelandic speakers pronounce it closer to <strong>HRAPBN</strong> (the <em>f</em> has become a stop). Many scholars reading aloud use "RAVN" for simplicity.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Etymology and Cognates</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> <em>Hrafn</em> descends from Proto-Germanic <em>*hrabnaz</em>, the reconstructed common ancestor of all the Germanic raven words. The cognates include:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Old English: <em>hræfn</em> (modern English "raven")</li>
      <li>Old High German: <em>hraban</em> / <em>raban</em> (modern German <em>Rabe</em>)</li>
      <li>Old Saxon: <em>hraban</em></li>
      <li>Gothic: <em>*hraban</em> (reconstructed)</li>
      <li>Modern Icelandic: <em>hrafn</em> (unchanged)</li>
      <li>Modern Norwegian / Danish: <em>ravn</em></li>
      <li>Modern Swedish: <em>korp</em> (from a different root — <em>*kruppaz</em>)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The root is likely imitative of the raven's call — a Proto-Indo-European root *ker- / *kor- associated with harsh crying sounds, related to Latin <em>corvus</em> (raven/crow) and Greek <em>korax</em> (raven).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Use in Odin's Titles and Names</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The word <em>hrafn</em> is central to Odin's raven-associations. His ravens are named <em>Huginn</em> ("Thought") and <em>Muninn</em> ("Memory"), recorded in <em>Grímnismál</em> stanza 20 (Codex Regius). Odin's by-names include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Hrafnaguð</em> — "raven-god" (genitive plural <em>hrafna-</em>)</li>
      <li><em>Hrafnáss</em> — "raven-Áss" (raven-god of the Æsir)</li>
      <li><em>Hrafnfreistuðr</em> — "raven-tester/tempter" (a more obscure byname)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These bynames are listed in the <em>Prose Edda</em> (Snorri Sturluson, c. 1220 CE) and in <em>þulur</em> (mnemonic lists of heiti — poetic synonyms) appended to the <em>Skáldskaparmál</em>.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Place Names and Personal Names</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> <em>Hrafn</em> is extremely common in Old Norse place names and personal names, reflecting the raven's prominence in Norse culture:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Personal names:</strong> Hrafnkel (raven-kettle; subject of <em>Hrafnkels saga</em>), Hrafn (a common male name), Hrafnhildr (female name)</li>
      <li><strong>Icelandic place names:</strong> Hrafnagjá (Raven Gorge), Hrafntinnusker (Raven Obsidian Rock)</li>
      <li><strong>Norwegian place names:</strong> Ramnes, Ramsberg (from <em>hrafn-</em>)</li>
      <li><strong>The navigator:</strong> Hrafna-Flóki (Raven-Flóki) — Flóki Vilgerðarson who used ravens to navigate to Iceland, recorded in <em>Landnámabók</em>. See: <a href="/lore/norse/raven-floki-and-iceland/">Raven-Flóki and Iceland</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>In Norse Poetry — Kennings for the Raven</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Old Norse skaldic poetry used <em>kennings</em> — compound periphrastic expressions — for the raven. Common raven kennings include:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><em>Yggs / Óðins gjóðr</em> — "Odin's hawk/kite"</li>
      <li><em>valkjósandi</em> — "chooser of the slain" (applied to ravens and also to valkyries)</li>
      <li><em>svart-fjaðraðr</em> — "black-feathered one"</li>
      <li><em>blóð-ari</em> — "blood-eagle" is a separate term; ravens in battle poetry are <em>valkyrjur hræs</em> (corpse-valkyries)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The raven also appears in the <em>beasts of battle</em> formula — the conventional trio of raven, eagle, and wolf who await a battlefield — found across Old Norse and Old English poetry. See: <a href="/lore/norse/odin-the-raven-god/">Odin the Raven God</a>.</p>
  </section>

